--TEST--
A closure parameter naming a class is checked for objecthood and nothing more, so every other representation refuses while a foreign class and a nullable one both accept — ADR 0007 § 2, ADR 0031 § 1
--FILE--
<?mwl
// The depth claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*, over the one argument
// shape the neighbouring `arr-a-mixed-or-nullable-callback-parameter-is-unchecked`
// case cannot reach: an **object**. `mwl_runtime::closure::check_param_tags`
// compares the four-bit representation a parameter erases to, and
// `mwl_ir::lower::param_tag_nibble` gives every class name — and `object`
// itself — the same nibble 7, because the tag carries no class label. So the
// accept/refuse line below is drawn around objecthood: every non-object
// representation is refused, and every declaration that erases to an object or
// to a tagged slot accepts, whichever class it names.
//
// `Core\Out::capture`'s `{through:}` is the vehicle because it is the one
// `Core` member that hands a callback an object — its own `Core\Cli\Text`
// carrier (spec § 12). A `callable` is opaque as to signature (ADR 0031 § 1),
// so nothing static stands between the option and what the closure declares,
// and this check is the whole of what stands there at run time.
//
// **The class half of that is a hole, not an answer.** A parameter naming the
// wrong class is accepted here and every later read through it is a
// fixed-offset load against a label that is not true — unlike ADR 0036 § 4's
// erased receiver, which defers to a name-keyed fetch precisely because it has
// no label to trust. `docs/adr/README.md` § *Decisions taken at project start*
// owns which boundary is to pay for that and why; this case pins the line as
// it is drawn today, and asserting what a wrong-class read answers is
// deliberately not part of it.
final class Marker {
    public string $name = "m";
}

// Each declaration is written twice, bare then nullable, so a pair that
// flipped fails rather than balancing a total. `y` is "the closure ran", `n`
// is "the call threw `LogicError`" — and the pairs are the carrier's own class
// first, then the five representations an object is not, then a class the
// argument is not an instance of, then the two erased spellings.
array<callable> $filters = [
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c) => $c,
    fn (?Core\Cli\Text $c) => $c,
    fn (string $c) => $c,
    fn (?string $c) => $c,
    fn (int $c) => $c,
    fn (?int $c) => $c,
    fn (bool $c) => $c,
    fn (?bool $c) => $c,
    fn (array<string> $c) => $c,
    fn (?array<string> $c) => $c,
    // `float` is the one representation with a widening rule (ADR 0007 § 2),
    // and it is a rule about an `int` or a `uint` arriving — an object is
    // refused here like any other mismatch.
    fn (float $c) => $c,
    fn (?float $c) => $c,
    // The class-blind pair: `Marker` shares no ancestry with the carrier and
    // is accepted anyway, its nibble being the same 7.
    fn (Marker $c) => $c,
    fn (?Marker $c) => $c,
    // ADR 0007 § 3's opaque top, and § 2's one unchecked position.
    fn (object $c) => $c,
    fn (mixed $c) => $c,
];

var $marks = "";
array<string> $said = [];
foreach ($filters as callable $filter) {
    try {
        var $answer = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "w"; }, {through: $filter});
        $marks = $marks . "y";
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        $marks = $marks . "n";
        $said[] = $refused->message;
    }
}
echo $marks, "\n";

// What each refusal names is the representation the parameter asked for and
// the one the argument carried — `object`, with no class in it either. Five
// refusals, in the order the sweep met them, so a message that grew a class
// name on one side would fail here rather than pass a count.
foreach ($said as string $line) {
    echo $line, "\n";
}

// Nothing the refused five captured reaches this program's output: the
// capture level is closed on both edges whichever way the `through` goes. What
// the accepted ones answer is the carrier itself, unconverted — the argument
// arrives whole under a declaration that names another class entirely, which
// is the same statement as the `y` above, said where it can be read.
echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "arrives whole under a foreign class"; }, {through: fn (Marker $c) => $c}), "\n";
echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "and under a nullable one"; }, {through: fn (?Marker $c) => $c}), "\n";
--EXPECT--
yynynynynynyyyyy
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type string, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type int, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type bool, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type array, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type float, object given
arrives whole under a foreign class
and under a nullable one
